WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.430
A truck for a village school can

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.370
carry a thousand kilos, and there

3
00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.190
are six loads to choose from.

4
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.960
Can you write a program that finds the

5
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.580
best load, and knows it’s the best?

6
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.180
Today: let the computer search, by trying

7
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.920
everything, or by thinking ahead.

8
00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.310
Each load has a weight and points for

9
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:25.590
how much the school needs it.

10
00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:27.940
Solar panels: six hundred

11
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.860
kilos, forty-two points.

12
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.420
Desks and books: five hundred kilos each.

13
00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.180
A water filter, footballs

14
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.460
and chalk are lighter.

15
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.270
The goal: the most points

16
00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.010
within a thousand kilos.

17
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.360
The simplest search tries

18
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.100
every possible load.

19
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:51.330
Each item is in or out, six times over.

20
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.840
That’s two to the six, sixty-four loads:

21
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.980
a phone checks them in a blink.

22
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.840
In Python, count from zero to

23
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.160
sixty-three, and read each number in

24
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.100
binary: bit i says whether item i is in.

25
00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.830
Add up the weight and points, and

26
00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.450
keep the best that fits.

27
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.640
But every extra item doubles the work.

28
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.270
Thirty items: over a billion loads.

29
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.540
Sixty items: more than a billion billion.

30
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.900
Brute force is sure, but it doesn’t scale.

31
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.980
A greedy rule is fast: take the

32
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.640
most points per kilo first.

33
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.560
Solar panels go in, then whatever

34
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.340
still fits: sixty points.

35
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.700
But desks and books give sixty-five.

36
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.770
The panels took space that two

37
00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:45.990
other loads fill better.

38
00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:47.850
Quick check!

39
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.280
Greedy says sixty points, and

40
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.420
brute force says sixty-five.

41
00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.330
Which one is guaranteed to be the best?

42
00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.530
Brute force: it checked every load.

43
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.070
But you can be sure without

44
00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.210
checking everything.

45
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.580
Dynamic programming solves smaller trucks

46
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.060
first: for each amount of room, either

47
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.120
skip this item, or take it and fill the

48
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.000
rest with an answer you already know.

49
00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.010
Fill a table: one row per item, one

50
00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.970
column per hundred kilos of room.

51
00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.230
Each cell needs just two

52
00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.430
lookups in the row above.

53
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.140
A hundred items is about a thousand cells,

54
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.880
where brute force would never finish.

55
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.760
The points are someone’s opinion

56
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.120
of what the school needs.

57
00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.670
Who decided solar panels

58
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.090
are worth forty-two?

59
00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:43.370
If the truck served two schools, the best

60
00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.770
total might send everything to one.

61
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.220
Ask who chose the points,

62
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.000
and who is left out.

63
00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.730
In the app, open “Pack the truck”.

64
00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:57.550
Pack greedily, then show the best load.

65
00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.730
Open the table under the

66
00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.890
results, and follow the outlined

67
00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.730
cells back from the corner.

68
00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:05.740
Your turn.

69
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.000
A friend adds a seventh load to the list.

70
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.440
How many loads must brute force check now?

71
00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:14.910
A hundred and twenty-eight:

72
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.250
every extra item doubles it.

73
00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.850
Remember: brute force is

74
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.490
sure, but it explodes.

75
00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.520
Greedy is fast, but it can miss.

76
00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.450
And dynamic programming is sure and fast,

77
00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.750
because it reuses smaller answers.

78
00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.770
Try it yourself: pack your

79
00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.010
bag for a school trip.

80
00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.870
Give each thing a weight and points, set a

81
00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.750
limit, and find the best load three ways.
